```

In the test class `ArrayListContractTest`, the attributes `[PexMethod]` and `[PexAssumeUnderTest]` on the tests indicates that the tests are contract-based tests and the target tested type is `[ArrayList arrList]` respectively. 

For each method in the ArrayList, we added a new contract test method, in which we make an educated guess based on some assumptions, call the method under test, and observe the post-condition. 

We're expecting that the counts and indices don't change unless we are actually adding to the middle. 

This code uses `PexObserve.ValueForViewing(string name, object value)` to output information that can be viewed in the test explorer.

The contracts are verified with the Pex framework that comes with Microsoft’s Test Development Kit (TDK, or VSTT) and includes support for testing .NET. 

The `PUT_ContainsContract` method checks the return value of `arrList.Contains(x)`. When the return value is `false`, it checks whether the index of `x` in the old list is more than the index of `x` in the new list. 

This provides a more comprehensive and meaningful contract that can validate ArrayList API in the contract testing.--------------------	    // PexObserve.Assert(@"If ret is False, then new arrList_LastIndexOf_x <= new arrList_IndexOf_x");
